.Sh DESCRIPTION
The ctrlres library implements routines to automate reading values from resource databases.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
function reads resources from a sequence of resource databases,
and call the corresponding setter function for each resource
to parse/convert it into relevant values
(integers, color, boolean, etc).
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_lookup
function reads a single resource from a sequence of resource databases,
and returns its (unparsed/unconverted) string value.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_istrue
function conveniently checks whether a string (likely read from a resource) is
.Dq true , "1" , "on" ,
or other values accepted as truth.
.Pp
The
.Fa setter
function is called by
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
to convert the read resource into a useful value
(integer, color, file path, etc).
Two setter functions are provided by
.Lb ctrlres :
.Fn CTRLRES_SETBOOL
and
.Fn CTRLRES_SETSTR ,
for boolean and string values, respectively.
.Pp
The
.Ft "struct ctrlres"
structure identifies a resource and how to parse it.
.Ss Parameters
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa ctx
Points to the object given to the
.Fa setter
function.
.It Fa name_path , class_path
The sequence of nodes forming the resource's instance path and class path.
.It Fa nnodes
The number of nodes in both
.Fa name_path
and
.Fa class_path
arrays (both have the same length).
.It Fa ndbs
The number of resource databases in the
.Fa dbs
array.
.It Fa value
The raw/unconverted resource read from the resource database.
.It Fa dbs
The array of X resource databases to read resources from.
.El
.Ss Data structure
The
.Ft "struct ctrlres"
structure contains the paths for a resource,
how to convert it into a relevant value,
and where to store the converted value at.
.Pp
Its members are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa addr
The address where the
.Fa setter
function will store the converted resource value.
.It Fa setter
The pointer to a function called by
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
to convert resources into a relevant value.
If it is
.Dv NULL ,
the resource is ignored, even if found in any database, and is not parsed.
The setter function must return nonzero if it could successfully parse the resource
and store the converted value into
.Fa addr ,
or return zero otherwise.
.It Fa name_path , class_path
The sequence of nodes forming the resource's instance path and class path.
.It Fa nnodes
The number of nodes in both
.Fa name_path
and
.Fa class_path
arrays (both have the same length).
.El
.Ss Callback function
The
.Fa setter
member is a pointer to a function called by
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
to
(a) convert the read resource into a value, and
(b) store the converted value at the address pointed to by
.Fa addr .
.Pp
The opaque pointer
.Fa ctx
is passed to the setter function, and can contain anything.
.Pp
The opaque pointer
.Fa addr ,
passed to the setter function,
contains the address where to store the converted resource value at.
This allows the same setter function to be reused for different,
but same-typed, resources.
.Ss Lookup functions
The
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
and
.Fn ctrlres_lookup
read resources from a sequence of resource databases.
.Pp
For each resource,
databases in the
.Fa dbs
array are checked in sequence until the resource is defined.
If it is found in a database, its value defined there is used,
and the remaining databases are ignored.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
function looks various resources up, as specified in the
.Fa resources
table.
For each resource found in the databases,
this function calls the resource's respective
.Fa setter
callback function to convert the resource's value,
and stores it into the respective address pointed to by
.Fa addr .
If
.Fa setter
allocates memory (and assign its address into
.Fa addr ) ,
it is up to the caller procedure to free it.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_lookup
function looks a single resource up, and returns its string value
(or
.Dv NULL ,
if the resource was not found in any database);
but do not convert it nor stores it anywhere.
The returning string resides in the database where the resource was read from,
and should not be freed,
nor should be used after the database is destroyed.
.Pp
Each resource is specified by a pair of same-length paths:
.Fa name_path
and
.Fa class_path ,
both made of
.Fa nnodes
nodes.
See
.Xr X 7 ,
at the section
.Sx RESOURCES
for a description on resource paths.
.Ss Setter functions
The
.Fn CTRLRES_SETBOOL
and
.Fn CTRLRES_SETSTR
functions are setters that can be used by
.Fn ctrlres_parsexrdb
for boolean and string resources.
.Pp
.Fn CTRLRES_SETBOOL
stores 1 into
.Fa addr ,
if
.Fa value
is either
.Dq true ,
.Dq on ,
.Dq yes ,
or
.Dq 1 .
And stores 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn CTRLRES_SETSTR
stores the address of the resource's value (a character string)
read from the database into
.Fa addr .
That string resides in the database, and should not be freed.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_istrue
function is used by the
.Fn CTRLRES_SETBOOL
setter to check whether a character string represents a truth value.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Fn ctrlres_lookup
function returns a pointer to the resource value string,
or
.Dv NULL
if the resource was not found.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlres_istrue
function returns 1 if
.Fa value
is either
.Dq true ,
.Dq on ,
.Dq yes ,
or
.Dq 1 ;
and returns 0 otherwise.
.Pp
The
.Fn CTRLRES_SETBOOL
and
.Fn CTRLRES_SETSTR
functions always return 1, indicating successfull parsing and storing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following code illustrates the parsing of resources for an application called
.Em XSample :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int (*parse_color)(void *ctx, void *addr, const char *value);
widget *widget;
XColor start_bg, start_fg, close_bg, close_fg;
Bool spellcheck;
char const *motd;

statc char const *APP_NAME  = "xsample"
statc char const *APP_CLASS = "XSample"
static struct ctrlres resources[] = {
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "startButton", "background"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "Button",      "Background"},
		.nnodes = 3,
		.addr = &start_bg,
		.setter = parse_color,
	},
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "startButton", "foreground"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "Button",      "Foreground"},
		.nnodes = 3,
		.addr = &start_fg,
		.setter = parse_color,
	},
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "closeButton", "background"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "Button",      "Background"},
		.nnodes = 3,
		.addr = &close_bg,
		.setter = parse_color,
	},
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "closeButton", "foreground"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "Button",      "Foreground"},
		.nnodes = 3,
		.addr = &close_fg,
		.setter = parse_color,
	},
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "enableSpelcheck"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "EnableSpelcheck"},
		.nnodes = 2,
		.addr = &spellcheck,
		.setter = CTRLRES_SETBOOL,
	},
	{
		.name_path  = (char *[]){APP_NAME,  "welcomeMessage"},
		.class_path = (char *[]){APP_CLASS, "WelcomeMessage"},
		.nnodes = 2,
		.addr = &motd,
		.setter = CTRLRES_SETSTR,
	},
};

ctrlres_parsexrdb(
	widget,
	(XrmDatabase[]){cli_db, runtime_db}, 2,
	resources, sizeof(resources)/sizeof(*resources)
);
.Ed
